---
// How many more miles the front of the aircraft costs.
//
// REBUILT 13 Aug 2026. The first version drew five zones x three cabins as
// fifteen bars, each scaled against the largest number in the whole chart. Two
// faults, and the reader named the first:
//
//   1. Fifteen rows to deliver one claim. The claim is a ratio, and a ratio is
//      a single number with a range around it.
//   2. Worse, the bars encoded ABSOLUTE miles while the argument is about
//      RATIOS. Scaled to a global maximum, Zone 5's bars looked short and Zone
//      11's long — so the picture said "Europe costs more than Shanghai", which
//      is true, irrelevant, and not what the surrounding text claims.
//
// Now: one zone drawn properly, with bars encoding the multiple so economy is
// always one unit and the shape IS the argument. The consistency claim is
// carried by a stated range, and the full table sits in a disclosure for anyone
// who wants to check it.
//
// The featured zone is Zone 10, chosen deliberately. It is long-haul (where the
// cash gap is widest and the argument strongest), it is a trip in our own
// ledger, and at 2.13x its business multiple sits BELOW the median of the five
// — so featuring it cannot be accused of cherry-picking the best case.
//
// Figures: one-way Saver awards from Singapore, chart effective 1 November 2025.
// Cross-checked across two independent transcriptions, with four cells since
// corroborated to the mile by our own booking confirmations.
import ToolBrand from '../ToolBrand.astro';

const ZONES = [
  { zone: 5,  label: 'Beijing, Shanghai',           y: 20500, j: 45000,  f: 61500  },
  { zone: 8,  label: 'Perth, Darwin',               y: 20500, j: 42500,  f: 60500  },
  { zone: 9,  label: 'Australia (others), NZ',      y: 29000, j: 72000,  f: 98000  },
  { zone: 10, label: 'Africa, Middle East, Turkey', y: 32000, j: 68000,  f: 95000  },
  { zone: 11, label: 'Europe',                      y: 44000, j: 108500, f: 148000 },
];

const rows = ZONES.map((z) => ({ ...z, jx: z.j / z.y, fx: z.f / z.y }));
const hero = rows.find((r) => r.zone === 10)!;

const jxLo = Math.min(...rows.map((r) => r.jx));
const jxHi = Math.max(...rows.map((r) => r.jx));
const fxLo = Math.min(...rows.map((r) => r.fx));
const fxHi = Math.max(...rows.map((r) => r.fx));

// Bars encode the MULTIPLE, not the mileage: economy is always one unit, and
// the widest bar in the group fills the track. The shape is the claim.
const scale = (mult: number) => (mult / hero.fx) * 100;

const n = (v: number) => v.toLocaleString('en-SG');
const x = (v: number) => v.toFixed(2) + '×';

const CABINS = [
  { key: 'Economy',       miles: hero.y, mult: 1,       cls: 'y' },
  { key: 'Business',      miles: hero.j, mult: hero.jx, cls: 'j' },
  { key: 'First / Suites',miles: hero.f, mult: hero.fx, cls: 'f' },
];
---

<div class="cl">
  <div class="cl-head">
    <span class="cl-lbl">What the front of the aircraft costs in miles</span>
    <span class="cl-lbl accent">Chart effective 1 Nov 2025</span>
  </div>

  <p class="cl-preset">One route, to make the point once. Singapore to Africa, one way, at the
  cheapest award tier. The bars show <b>how many times the economy price</b> each cabin costs
  &mdash; not the mileage, which is printed beside them.</p>

  <div class="cl-hero">
    <div class="cl-zone"><b>Singapore &rarr; Africa, Middle East or Turkey</b><em>Zone {hero.zone} &middot; one-way Saver award</em></div>
    {CABINS.map((c) => (
      <div class="cl-bar">
        <div class="cl-bar-l">
          <span>{c.key}</span>
          <b class="num">{n(c.miles)}{' '}<i>{x(c.mult)} the economy price</i></b>
        </div>
        <div class="cl-track"><div class={`cl-fill ${c.cls}`} style={`width:${scale(c.mult)}%`}></div></div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="cl-out">
    <div class="cl-out-s">Business costs about <b>twice</b> the miles of economy. A Suite,
    about <b>three times</b>. That is the whole point of this chart &mdash; and the reason it matters is
    that <b>cash fares do not work that way</b>. On a long-haul route the business ticket is a far
    bigger multiple of the economy fare than two.</div>
  </div>

  <details class="cl-more">
    <summary><b>The same pattern across five zones</b> &mdash; it is a rule, not a quirk of one route</summary>
    <p class="cl-more-p">Business runs <b>{x(jxLo)}&ndash;{x(jxHi)}</b> the economy price across the
    zones in this article, and First or Suites <b>{x(fxLo)}&ndash;{x(fxHi)}</b>. The route above
    sits below the middle of that range, so it is not the flattering case.</p>
    <div class="cl-tblwrap">
      <table class="cl-tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Zone</th><th>Economy</th><th>Business</th><th>First / Suites</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr class={r.zone === hero.zone ? 'hero' : ''}>
              <td><b>{r.zone}</b><em>{r.label}</em></td>
              <td class="num">{n(r.y)}<em>1.00×</em></td>
              <td class="num">{n(r.j)}<em>{x(r.jx)}</em></td>
              <td class="num">{n(r.f)}<em>{x(r.fx)}</em></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <div class="cl-notes">
    <p><b>What this is.</b> Singapore Airlines&rsquo; own one-way Saver award pricing, at the
    cheapest tier. Premium economy is left out for legibility, not for judgement.</p>
    <p><b>What it is not.</b> A claim about availability. Saver seats are the cheapest rung and the
    scarcest &mdash; a published price you cannot book is worth nothing, and no chart shows how
    many seats sit behind each number.</p>
    <ToolBrand />
  </div>
</div>

<style is:global>
.cl{
  --cl-bd:var(--color-border); --cl-t1:var(--color-dark); --cl-t2:var(--color-body);
  --cl-t3:var(--color-muted); --cl-deep:var(--color-navy);
  --cl-mono:var(--font-mono),'SF Mono',Menlo,Consolas,monospace;
  background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cl-bd);border-radius:12px;
  box-shadow:0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,.05),0 2px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.04);
  overflow:hidden;margin:26px 0;font-size:16px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--cl-t1)
}
.cl .num{font-family:var(--cl-mono);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.cl-head{padding:14px 22px;background:var(--color-surface);border-bottom:1px solid var(--cl-bd);
  display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.cl-lbl{font-size:11px;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;color:var(--cl-t3)}
.cl-lbl.accent{color:var(--color-brand-700)}
.post-content .cl-preset{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--cl-t3);line-height:1.55;
  margin:0;padding:12px 22px;border-bottom:1px dashed var(--cl-bd)}
.cl-preset b{color:var(--cl-t2);font-weight:600}

.cl-hero{padding:16px 22px 18px}
.cl-zone{margin-bottom:14px}
.cl-zone b{font-size:14.5px;font-weight:700;color:var(--cl-t1)}
.cl-zone em{display:block;font-style:normal;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cl-t3);margin-top:1px}
.cl-bar{margin-bottom:14px}
.cl-bar:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
.cl-bar-l{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;margin-bottom:5px}
.cl-bar-l span{font-size:14px;color:var(--cl-t2)}
.cl-bar-l b{font-family:var(--cl-mono);font-size:15px;font-weight:600;color:var(--cl-t1)}
.cl-bar-l i{font-style:normal;font-family:var(--font-body);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;
  color:var(--cl-t3);margin-left:9px}
.cl-track{height:13px;background:var(--color-surface-alt);border-radius:999px;overflow:hidden}
.cl-fill{height:100%;border-radius:999px;min-width:3px}
.cl-fill.y{background:#B9C4D2}
.cl-fill.j{background:var(--color-brand-600)}
.cl-fill.f{background:#9C7526}

.cl-out{background:var(--cl-deep);color:#fff;padding:17px 22px}
.cl-out-s{font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:#D8DEE8}
.cl-out-s b{font-weight:600;color:#fff}

.cl-more{border-top:1px solid var(--cl-bd);background:var(--color-surface)}
.cl-more > summary{cursor:pointer;padding:12px 22px;font-size:13px;color:var(--cl-t2);
  list-style:none;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px}
.cl-more > summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none}
.cl-more > summary::before{content:'+';font-family:var(--cl-mono);font-weight:700;
  color:var(--color-brand-700);font-size:15px;line-height:1}
.cl-more[open] > summary::before{content:'\2212'}
.cl-more > summary b{color:var(--cl-t1);font-weight:700}
.cl-more > summary:hover{color:var(--color-brand-700)}
.post-content .cl-more-p{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--cl-t3);line-height:1.55;margin:0;padding:0 22px 10px}
.cl-more-p b{color:var(--cl-t2);font-weight:600}
.cl-tblwrap{overflow-x:auto;padding:0 22px 16px}
.post-content .cl-tbl{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px;margin:0;min-width:420px}
.post-content .cl-tbl th{font-size:10.5px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.7px;
  color:#C9D3E0;font-weight:700;text-align:right;padding:7px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--cl-bd)}
.post-content .cl-tbl th:first-child{text-align:left;color:#fff}
.post-content .cl-tbl td{padding:8px 10px;border-bottom:1px dashed var(--cl-bd);text-align:right;
  color:var(--cl-t2);vertical-align:top}
.post-content .cl-tbl td:first-child{text-align:left}
.cl-tbl td b{font-size:13px;color:var(--cl-t1)}
.cl-tbl td em{display:block;font-style:normal;font-size:10.5px;color:var(--cl-t3);margin-top:1px;
  font-family:var(--font-body)}
.cl-tbl tr.hero td{background:rgba(200,154,60,.10)}

.cl-notes{padding:15px 22px 17px;border-top:1px solid var(--cl-bd);background:var(--color-surface)}
.post-content .cl-notes p{font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--cl-t3);margin:0 0 7px}
.post-content .cl-notes p:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
.cl-notes b{color:var(--cl-t2)}
@media(max-width:640px){
  .post-content .cl-tbl{min-width:0;font-size:12px}
  .post-content .cl-tbl th,.post-content .cl-tbl td{padding:6px 7px}
  .cl-hero{padding:14px 16px 16px}
  .cl-tblwrap{padding:0 16px 14px}
  .cl-more > summary{padding:12px 16px}
  .post-content .cl-more-p{padding:0 16px 10px}
}
</style>
